        {"title": "Trust in the Age of Technology", "intro": "Digital access has created new forms of both trust violation and surveillance that relationships are still learning to navigate.", "sections": [{"heading": "The Transparency Conversation", "body": ["Open phone policies, location sharing, shared passwords — these are tools that mean different things in different relationships and at different stages. What matters is explicit agreement rather than assumption.", "Surveillance without agreement is not transparency — it's monitoring. The difference is consent and purpose."]}], "callout": "Transparency is an agreement. Surveillance is a symptom."},
