        {"title": "Creating Safety First", "intro": "Rebuilding capacity for intimacy after trauma begins with safety, not with intimacy itself.", "sections": [{"heading": "The Foundation Before the Building", "body": ["Nervous system safety — the body's experience of being genuinely un-threatened — is the prerequisite for intimacy after trauma. Safety is built through: consistent, predictable partner behavior, explicit negotiation of boundaries, the ability to stop any intimate activity at any point without consequence, and gradual exposure to connection that never exceeds what feels safe."]}], "callout": "Safety first. Intimacy follows safety, not the other way around."},
        {"title": "Communication About History", "intro": "How and whether to share trauma history with a partner is a deeply personal and consequential decision.", "sections": [{"heading": "The Disclosure Question", "body": ["You are not obligated to share your trauma history with a partner, particularly early in a relationship. You are responsible for communicating current needs and limits.", "'I'm working through some things and I need [specific accommodation or behavior]' communicates need without requiring full history.", "As trust deepens, fuller sharing may feel right and may be received in ways that are profoundly healing — or not, if the partner is not capable of receiving it well. Timing and recipient selection matter enormously."]}], "callout": "You own your story. Share it on your terms."},
        {"title": "Partners of Trauma Survivors", "intro": "Loving someone with trauma history requires specific knowledge and specific practices.", "sections": [{"heading": "What Helps and What Doesn't", "body": ["Helpful: consistent patience, following the survivor's lead about pace and limits, educating yourself about trauma responses, maintaining your own support and processing outside the relationship.", "Unhelpful: taking trauma responses personally, pressuring for intimacy beyond the survivor's current capacity, interpreting caution as rejection, or making the survivor responsible for managing your frustration about the situation."]}], "callout": "Following the survivor's lead is the most important thing a partner can do."},
        {"title": "Somatic Approaches to Healing Intimacy", "intro": "Trauma in the body responds to body-based approaches that purely cognitive work cannot fully reach.", "sections": [{"heading": "The Body-Based Path", "body": ["Somatic therapies — including somatic experiencing, EMDR, and sensorimotor psychotherapy — address trauma at the nervous system level where it lives.", "Body-based self-care practices that support trauma healing: breathwork that builds tolerance for sensation, gentle movement that builds body awareness and safety, gradual and consensual touch experiences that build new associations."]}], "callout": "Trauma lives in the body. Healing reaches into the body too."},
